1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,200
At 3:12 a.m., the whole world falls asleep.

2
00:00:03,450 --> 00:00:04,250
Except you.

3
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:11,699
By day 3, you'll find out why nobody can wake up, and why you never should have tried.

4
00:00:14,549 --> 00:00:16,549
It starts with something small.

5
00:00:16,799 --> 00:00:18,399
You wake up thirsty.

6
00:00:18,649 --> 00:00:24,250
You walk to the kitchen, pour a glass of water, and you notice it:

7
00:00:24,500 --> 00:00:28,100
the fridge hum, the traffic outside, the neighbor's TV.

8
00:00:28,350 --> 00:00:29,950
Everything is still running.

9
00:00:30,200 --> 00:00:33,000
It's just that nobody is doing anything.

10
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:36,649
So here's what we're going to do.

11
00:00:36,899 --> 00:00:48,099
We're going to follow you, hour by hour, for the next 72 hours, and see exactly how long the world keeps going without anyone awake to run it.

12
00:00:50,949 --> 00:00:53,749
You go to your brother's room first.

13
00:00:53,999 --> 00:00:55,599
You say his name.

14
00:00:55,849 --> 00:00:57,449
Then you shout it.

15
00:00:57,699 --> 00:00:59,699
Then you shake him, hard.

16
00:00:59,949 --> 00:01:00,749
He's breathing.

17
00:01:00,999 --> 00:01:05,000
His eyes are moving under his eyelids like he's dreaming.

18
00:01:05,250 --> 00:01:07,250
But he won't wake up.

19
00:01:08,099 --> 00:01:10,099
Your parents are the same.

20
00:01:10,349 --> 00:01:14,349
Your mom fell asleep mid-scroll, phone still in her hand.

21
00:01:14,599 --> 00:01:16,199
You try cold water.

22
00:01:16,449 --> 00:01:20,049
You try an alarm right next to her ear.

23
00:01:20,299 --> 00:01:23,899
You try the thing you swore you'd never do:

24
00:01:24,149 --> 00:01:25,749
you pinch her, hard.

25
00:01:26,599 --> 00:01:26,999
Nothing.

26
00:01:27,849 --> 00:01:29,049
You call 911.

27
00:01:29,299 --> 00:01:30,099
It rings.

28
00:01:30,349 --> 00:01:31,149
And rings.

29
00:01:31,399 --> 00:01:32,199
And rings.

30
00:01:32,449 --> 00:01:40,849
Somewhere, in a dispatch center, a phone is lighting up on a desk in front of someone who is fast asleep.

31
00:01:43,699 --> 00:01:44,899
You go outside.

32
00:01:45,149 --> 00:01:47,949
It's quieter than any city should be.

33
00:01:48,199 --> 00:01:56,999
A car has drifted into a mailbox at the end of the street, engine still running, the driver slumped over the wheel.

34
00:01:57,249 --> 00:01:57,649
Asleep.

35
00:01:57,899 --> 00:01:58,699
Not hurt.

36
00:01:58,949 --> 00:01:59,749
Just gone.

37
00:02:00,599 --> 00:02:01,799
Gas station clerk.

38
00:02:02,049 --> 00:02:02,449
Asleep.

39
00:02:02,699 --> 00:02:03,499
Security guard.

40
00:02:03,749 --> 00:02:04,149
Asleep.

41
00:02:04,399 --> 00:02:06,799
But here's the first weird thing:

42
00:02:07,049 --> 00:02:08,649
the dogs are awake.

43
00:02:08,899 --> 00:02:10,499
Every animal is awake.

44
00:02:10,749 --> 00:02:11,949
It's only people.

45
00:02:12,799 --> 00:02:15,599
Whatever this is, it happened to humans.

46
00:02:15,849 --> 00:02:17,049
All of them.

47
00:02:17,299 --> 00:02:18,499
All at once.

48
00:02:21,349 --> 00:02:26,549
By sunrise you're on a rooftop looking up, and you see a plane.

49
00:02:26,799 --> 00:02:29,199
It's flying perfectly straight, perfectly level.

50
00:02:29,449 --> 00:02:31,449
And that's what scares you.

51
00:02:32,299 --> 00:02:35,899
Planes don't need pilots to stay in the air.

52
00:02:36,149 --> 00:02:40,149
The autopilot will hold its course and altitude for hours.

53
00:02:40,399 --> 00:02:45,199
The problem is it'll keep holding them until the fuel runs out.

54
00:02:45,449 --> 00:02:47,449
This has actually happened before:

55
00:02:47,699 --> 00:02:56,499
in 2005, a passenger jet over Greece flew on autopilot for hours after its crew passed out, until the fuel ran out.

56
00:02:57,349 --> 00:03:01,349
Right now there are thousands of planes in the sky.

57
00:03:01,599 --> 00:03:05,999
And every single one of them has a clock on it.

58
00:03:06,849 --> 00:03:08,849
You don't watch the end.

59
00:03:09,099 --> 00:03:09,899
You can't.

60
00:03:12,749 --> 00:03:15,949
At hour 14, the lights start going out.

61
00:03:16,799 --> 00:03:20,399
The power grid isn't a machine that runs itself.

62
00:03:20,649 --> 00:03:27,849
People balance it all day, every day, matching how much power gets made to how much gets used.

63
00:03:28,099 --> 00:03:34,099
With nobody at the controls, small faults stop getting fixed, and they start to spread.

64
00:03:34,949 --> 00:03:36,949
But one building stays lit.

65
00:03:37,199 --> 00:03:37,999
The hospital.

66
00:03:38,249 --> 00:03:39,049
Backup generators.

67
00:03:39,299 --> 00:03:45,699
And that's when the real problem hits you, the one that's been sitting under everything else.

68
00:03:48,549 --> 00:03:50,549
Remember that glass of water?

69
00:03:50,799 --> 00:03:53,599
You woke up because you were thirsty.

70
00:03:53,849 --> 00:03:59,849
Everyone else is going to be thirsty too, and they can't get up to drink.

71
00:04:00,699 --> 00:04:04,699
A person can usually survive about three days without water.

72
00:04:04,949 --> 00:04:08,949
That means you don't have forever to figure this out.

73
00:04:09,199 --> 00:04:13,599
You have 72 hours, and 20 of them are already gone.

74
00:04:14,449 --> 00:04:16,449
So you make a decision.

75
00:04:16,699 --> 00:04:18,299
You can't save everyone.

76
00:04:18,549 --> 00:04:20,949
But maybe you can save them.

77
00:04:23,799 --> 00:04:36,999
Getting three sleeping people to a hospital, alone, takes you nine hours, a shopping cart, a wheelchair, and one very bad decision involving a delivery van you definitely don't know how to drive.

78
00:04:37,849 --> 00:04:40,649
The nurses are asleep at their stations.

79
00:04:40,899 --> 00:04:53,699
You find a poster on the wall about setting up an IV drip, and you watch a training video on one of the computers, over and over, until your hands stop shaking.

80
00:04:54,550 --> 00:04:59,350
By midnight, your family is in three beds, on fluids, breathing steady.

81
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:01,200
You bought them time.

82
00:05:01,450 --> 00:05:02,650
Not a cure.

83
00:05:02,900 --> 00:05:03,300
Time.

84
00:05:06,150 --> 00:05:08,150
That's when your phone rings.

85
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:10,600
You stare at it.

86
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:15,250
Every phone on Earth is ringing right now, because nobody's answering.

87
00:05:15,500 --> 00:05:17,500
But this one's calling you.

88
00:05:18,350 --> 00:05:19,550
You pick up.

89
00:05:19,800 --> 00:05:20,200
Static.

90
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:23,250
And then, under the static, a voice.

91
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:31,500
You can't make out the words, only that it's a woman's voice, and that she sounds like she's been crying.

92
00:05:32,350 --> 00:05:33,550
The line cuts.

93
00:05:33,800 --> 00:05:35,000
You call back.

94
00:05:35,250 --> 00:05:37,650
This number is not in service.

95
00:05:40,500 --> 00:05:41,300
Day 3.

96
00:05:41,550 --> 00:05:44,750
You've read everything you can find about sleep.

97
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:47,800
And there's one thing you haven't tried:

98
00:05:48,050 --> 00:05:49,650
not noise, not pain.

99
00:05:49,900 --> 00:05:50,700
Something familiar.

100
00:05:50,950 --> 00:05:53,350
Something only that person would recognize.

101
00:05:54,200 --> 00:05:56,600
You play your brother a song.

102
00:05:56,850 --> 00:06:01,250
The stupid one you two always fought over in the car.

103
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:04,700
You hum the part he always got wrong.

104
00:06:05,550 --> 00:06:06,750
His eyes open.

105
00:06:07,600 --> 00:06:09,600
He looks right at you.

106
00:06:09,850 --> 00:06:14,650
And he says the strangest thing anyone has ever said to you:

107
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,100
"Why are you here?

108
00:06:17,350 --> 00:06:20,950
You're supposed to be the one in the bed."

109
00:06:23,800 --> 00:06:26,200
And the walls start to melt.

110
00:06:27,050 --> 00:06:27,850
Not fall.

111
00:06:28,100 --> 00:06:29,700
Melt, like wet paint.

112
00:06:29,950 --> 00:06:33,950
The ward, the beds, the window, the city behind it.

113
00:06:34,200 --> 00:06:37,800
Because you finally understand why nobody could wake up.

114
00:06:38,650 --> 00:06:40,250
They were never asleep.

115
00:06:40,500 --> 00:06:41,300
You were.

116
00:06:42,150 --> 00:06:50,950
At 3:12 a.m., three days ago, you got up for a glass of water, and you never made it back to bed.

117
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:58,800
Everything since then, the empty streets, the planes, the lights going out, has been happening inside your own head.

118
00:06:59,050 --> 00:07:10,250
And the voice on the phone was your mom, sitting next to you, holding the phone to your ear because a doctor told her familiar voices might help.

119
00:07:11,100 --> 00:07:15,500
And that's why you never should have tried to wake them.

120
00:07:15,750 --> 00:07:21,750
Every time you reached one of them, you were really reaching for the way out.

121
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:34,800
And the way out means leaving this world behind, the one where you were the only one who mattered, and going back to the one where you're the one who needs help.

122
00:07:37,650 --> 00:07:42,050
So here's the choice the whole video has been building to.

123
00:07:42,300 --> 00:07:50,700
Stay in a world where you're the only one awake, a world that's slowly running out of power, water and people.

124
00:07:50,950 --> 00:08:00,150
Or take his hand, and wake up into a hospital bed, with a long recovery and everyone you love crying at your bedside.

125
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:02,600
You take his hand.

126
00:08:03,450 --> 00:08:09,850
And somewhere, in a real hospital, at 3:12 in the afternoon, a phone hits the floor.

127
00:08:10,700 --> 00:08:12,700
Tell me in the comments:

128
00:08:12,950 --> 00:08:14,550
would you have stayed?

129
00:08:14,800 --> 00:08:25,600
And if you want to see what happens when the opposite happens, when everyone else wakes up and you're the one who can't, that's the next one.
